@article{ WOS:001730353300001,
Author = {Rojas-VeraPinto, Roxana and Bernardo-Madrid, Ruben and Gonzalez-Suarez,
   Manuela},
Title = {Data gaps and heterogeneity limit our understanding of human-wildlife
   interactions: A continental study of Andean bears},
Journal = {PEOPLE AND NATURE},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {8},
Number = {5},
Pages = {1499-1519},
Month = {MAY},
Abstract = {The expansion and intensification of human activities have increased
   interactions between people and wildlife. Interactions involving bears
   and other large carnivores are complex and can lead to conflicts.
   Promoting positive coexistence requires managing information, which is
   not always available. Our understanding of these interactions is
   particularly limited in South America, where human activities are
   rapidly encroaching on carnivore habitats, increasing the potential for
   conflict. We addressed this knowledge gap by conducting a systematic
   review of reported human-Andean bear interactions (HABI) in the English
   and Spanish peer-reviewed and grey literature. We created a dataset of
   events and summarized the state of knowledge mapping and characterizing
   the reported information. To explore the potential to predict
   interactions, we modelled the association between reported events and
   environmental and socioeconomic variables. Our review identified 96
   documents reporting 1110 HABI events across all five countries where
   Andean bears occur, but only covering 184 political units, approximately
   11\% of their distribution range. Most documents (83\%) reported events
   communicated by people, rather than directly observed or field-inspected
   interactions. Half of the documents were grey literature published in
   Spanish. The most common events reported were interactions with domestic
   animals and crops (reported in 121 units) and hunting of bears by humans
   (84 units) with very rare reports of attacks or collisions with vehicles
   (three units). How much information was provided for reported events
   varied widely and around two-thirds of events did not provide key
   information including specific locations and dates. Guidance for HABI
   management was also inconsistent. We located 17 conservation plans
   offering various mitigation actions but limited evidence of their
   effectiveness. Reported HABI events were only weakly associated with
   tested environmental and socioeconomic variables. Our study reveals a
   large focus on negative HABI across South America, and also a lack of
   key details that limits our ability to understand and anticipate
   potential interactions and conflicts. Information was very limited or
   not available for several regions and types of interactions. Improving
   our understanding of human-Andean bear interactions requires a broader
   scope with more consistent and standardized event reporting to best
   support effective coexistence and conservation.Read the free for this
   article on the Journal blog.
   La expansi \& oacute;n e intensificaci \& oacute;n de las actividades
   humanas han propiciado un aumento de las interacciones entre las
   personas y la fauna silvestre. Estas interacciones pueden ser m \&
   aacute;s complejas y llevar a conflictos cuando involucran a osos y
   otros grandes carn \& iacute;voros. Promover una coexistencia positiva
   requiere disponer de informaci \& oacute;n, la cual no siempre est \&
   aacute; disponible. Nuestro conocimiento de estas interacciones es
   particularmente limitado en Am \& eacute;rica del Sur, donde la expansi
   \& oacute;n de actividades humanas est \& aacute; invadiendo r \&
   aacute;pidamente los h \& aacute;bitats de los carn \& iacute;voros y
   aumentando el potencial de conflicto. Abordamos este vac \& iacute;o de
   conocimiento mediante una revisi \& oacute;n sistem \& aacute;tica de
   las interacciones entre humanos y osos andinos (HABI) descritas en
   documentos en ingl \& eacute;s y espa \& ntilde;ol de fuentes revisadas
   por pares y literatura gris. Generamos una base de datos sobre los
   eventos reportados y resumimos el estado del conocimiento, mapeando y
   caracterizando la informaci \& oacute;n descrita. Para explorar el
   potencial de predecir las interacciones, modelamos la asociaci \&
   oacute;n entre los eventos reportados y algunas variables ambientales y
   socioecon \& oacute;micas. Nuestra revisi \& oacute;n identific \&
   oacute; 96 documentos, los cuales reportan 1110 eventos en los cinco pa
   \& iacute;ses donde el oso andino est \& aacute; presente. Estos eventos
   solo cubrieron 184 unidades pol \& iacute;ticas, aproximadamente el 11\%
   del \& aacute;rea de distribuci \& oacute;n total de la especie. La
   mayor \& iacute;a de los documentos (83\%) reportan eventos comunicados
   por las personas, en lugar de observaciones directas o inspecciones en
   el campo. La mitad de los documentos son de la literatura gris publicada
   en espa \& ntilde;ol. Los eventos m \& aacute;s com \& uacute;nmente
   reportados fueron las interacciones con animales dom \& eacute;sticos y
   cultivos (reportados en 121 unidades pol \& iacute;ticas) y la caza de
   osos (84 unidades), siendo raros los reportes de ataques o colisiones
   con veh \& iacute;culos (tres unidades). La cantidad de informaci \&
   oacute;n proporcionada sobre los eventos vari \& oacute; ampliamente,
   habiendo alrededor de dos tercios de eventos que no reportaron informaci
   \& oacute;n clave como localidades y fechas detalladas. Las indicaciones
   para la gesti \& oacute;n de los HABI tambi \& eacute;n fueron
   inconsistentes. Identificamos 17 planes de conservaci \& oacute;n que
   ofrecen diversas medidas de mitigaci \& oacute;n, pero con pruebas
   limitadas de su eficacia. Finalmente, los eventos reportados estuvieron
   d \& eacute;bilmente asociados a variables ambientales y socioecon \&
   oacute;micas. Nuestro estudio revela un mayor \& eacute;nfasis en las
   interacciones negativas en toda Sudam \& eacute;rica y una falta de
   detalles clave en los reportes los cuales limitan nuestra capacidad para
   comprender y anticipar posibles interacciones y conflictos. La informaci
   \& oacute;n es muy limitada o no est \& aacute; disponible para varias
   regiones y tipos de interacciones. Para mejorar nuestra comprensi \&
   oacute;n de estas interacciones es necesario mejorar el reporte de
   eventos de forma m \& aacute;s coherente y estandarizada, con el fin de
   apoyar efectivamente la coexistencia y conservaci \& oacute;n.},
